title: os.pidfd_open is absent from this interpreter, so hark's production pidfd path never runs and its test coverage is illusory
updated_at: "2026-10-26T00:13:15Z"
---
Found while root-causing B184. Confirmed independently by the coordinator 2026-08-23.

    $ uv run python -c "import os,sys; print(sys.version); print(hasattr(os,\"pidfd_open\"))"
    3.13.11 (main, Jan 28 2026, 00:01:45) [Clang 21.1.4]
    False

os.pidfd_open does not exist in the uv-managed CPython used for this project (python-build-standalone, Clang build). It is a Linux-only CPython API added in 3.9 and it is genuinely missing here, not merely unusable.

Why this matters more than it looks:
1. Every pidfd code path in hark falls to its non-pidfd fallback on this machine, always. The pidfd branch has never executed here.
2. Therefore the pidfd-named tests in tests/test_tts_interrupt.py (test_outer_pidfd_is_owned_before_delivered_sigint, test_pipe_creation_publishes_both_fds_before_delivered_sigint, and the pidfd ownership family generally) are NOT exercising the pidfd implementation. Whatever they assert, they assert about the fallback. Coverage that reads as "pidfd is tested" is illusory.
3. This is B184 mechanism (a)-adjacent: part of why that family behaves oddly is that its subject does not exist in this interpreter.

To decide (this is why it is filed rather than fixed):
- Is the pidfd path meant to be reachable in production at all? If deployments use a distro CPython that HAS pidfd_open, then hark ships a path that is live for users and untested everywhere it is developed - the worse of the two cases.
- If so, the pidfd tests need to be either skipped explicitly with a reason (so the gap is visible rather than silently passing against the fallback), or run against an interpreter that has the symbol - a CI matrix entry, or a distro python.
- If not - if the fallback is the only path that ever runs - then the pidfd code should go, along with the tests naming it.

Either answer is fine. The current state, where the tests pass and nobody knows they are testing the fallback, is not.

Check before assuming: verify on the deployment target as well as here. `python -c "import os; print(hasattr(os, \"pidfd_open\"))"` on a distro CPython 3.13 will likely print True, which is exactly the mismatch that makes this worth resolving.